## Review gate

On: Claude's pushes of a commit with no `Verdict: pass` record under `.scratch/reviews/` are refused. Run the branch
through the `cross-review` skill before pushing. A fresh clone has the gate off until `.scratch/reviews/` is created
at its root.
{%- endif %}
{%- if gate == 'python' %}

## Quality gate

`scripts/gate [BASE]` runs the tests with coverage and octoflow-ai's `gate.py` over the change since BASE (default
the branch `main`), writing `quality.md`. Run it before handing a branch to review; fix each issue it lists on changed
lines, and fix or justify each finding to weigh. A change to docs alone (`docs/` and top-level Markdown) runs the
gate's linters and no tests.
{%- elif gate == 'own' %}

## Quality gate

`scripts/gate [BASE]` is this repo's own quality gate over the change since BASE (default the branch `main`); its
header says what it checks and what CI expects of it. Run it before handing a branch to review, and fix what it reports.
{%- endif %}
{%- if ci != 'none' %}

## CI and merging

{% if ci == 'jenkins' -%}
Jenkins builds every pull request and `main` with the `Jenkinsfile`, running `scripts/gate` over a pull request since
its merge base and over `main` since its last successful build, and reports `continuous-integration/jenkins/pr-head`
on each pull request through its GitHub App.
{%- if release %}
A build of `main` with no earlier success to start from judges everything since the release before its commit, or the
whole tree before the first release.
{%- endif %}
{%- else -%}
The Gate workflow (`.github/workflows/gate.yml`) runs `scripts/gate` from the merge base on every pull request into
`main`, as the check `gate`.
{%- if release %}
It also runs on each push to `main`, one at a time, judging everything since the last run there that passed; with
none, since the release before that push, or the whole tree before the first release.
{%- endif %}
{%- endif %}
{%- if automerge %}

Once that passes on a pull request's head commit, `.github/workflows/automerge.yml` merges it into `main` with a merge
commit and deletes its branch: opening a pull request is handing it over. To keep one for a human, open it as a draft
or label it `no-automerge`. It merges whatever files the pull request changes, CI's, the gate's and its own
configuration included; the header of `automerge.yml` is the rule, and names the `AUTOMERGE_TOKEN` secret it merges
with. Never merge a pull request yourself. A pull request that falls behind `main` is brought up to date by
auto-merge, which merges `main` into its branch, so pull before pushing more to one.
{%- else %}

Pull requests are merged by a human.
{%- endif %}
{%- endif %}
{%- if release %}

## Releases

{% if ci == 'jenkins' -%}
A merge into `main` releases the version as GitHub release `v<version>`, with whatever `scripts/package` leaves in
`dist/release/`, if that release doesn't exist yet (`scripts/release publish`, run by Jenkins's Release stage).
{%- else -%}
A merge into `main` releases the version as GitHub release `v<version>`, with whatever `scripts/package` leaves in
`dist/release/`, if that release doesn't exist yet (`scripts/release publish`, run by the Gate workflow's release
job once the gate passes on `main`).
{%- endif %}
The version is read from the first of `pyproject.toml`, `Cargo.toml`, `package.json` or `VERSION` that holds one
(`scripts/release`'s header). So a change reaches users only if it raises the version.

- Every change to a released path raises the version in the same pull request, and every spec or ticket for one lists
  the bump and its level as an acceptance criterion: `patch` for a fix, `minor` for a new feature or flag, `major` for
  a change that breaks what users rely on. {% if ci == 'jenkins' %}Jenkins's Version stage{% else %}The Gate workflow{% endif %} fails a pull request that doesn't.
{%- if automerge %}
  Auto-merge brings a pull request that is behind `main` up to date first, so the check runs again against `main` as
  it is when merging.
{%- endif %}
- While the version is 0.x, a breaking change bumps `minor`: `major` would make it 1.0.0, a deliberate release
  decision rather than a side effect of a change.
- Changes only to exempt paths (docs, tests, CI, repository tooling: `scripts/release`'s header lists them, plus the
  repo's own `.release-exempt`) don't bump the version, and build no package.
{%- if gate == 'python' %}
- Bump with `uv version --bump <level>`, never by editing `pyproject.toml`: it also updates `uv.lock`.
{%- else %}
- Bump with the tool `scripts/release`'s header names beside the file that holds the version.
{%- endif %}
- Don't create tags or releases by hand; CI does that after the merge.
{%- endif %}
